// The embedded bitmap font (Polyp v3 stage 2, UTF-8 in v4 stage 2).
//
// A 5x7 face for printable ASCII 32..126, drawn by hand for this stage from the same lattice a
// public-domain 5x7 face would use: five columns and seven rows per glyph, one byte per column
// in `FONT`, with bit `r` of a column byte the pixel at row `r` and row 0 at the top. Nothing
// here is copied from a licensed font. A few glyphs leave their rightmost column blank
// (`.` `,` `:` `;` `!`) but the pen step is still six columns, so the spacing is uniform.
//
// A glyph cell is 5x7 and the pen advances 6 columns. Polyp v4 stage 2 made the text UTF-8: a
// string is decoded into code points (`soft/utf8.kp`), each one cell wide, and `text_width` counts
// them. A malformed sequence is one replacement box per malformed run. Glyphs exist for ASCII, the
// whole of Latin-1 Supplement except the three vulgar fractions (U+00BC..U+00BE), the Latin
// Extended-A letters Polish/Czech/Slovak/Hungarian/Turkish/Baltic/Romanian text use (not the
// letters whose ascender leaves no room for a mark: ď ť ľ ĺ ĥ and friends), Greek capitals and
// lowercase (no tonos forms), the Cyrillic capitals and lowercase of the Russian alphabet with Ё/ё,
// the euro sign, the punctuation – — ‘ ’ ‚ “ ” „ † ‡ • …, the arrows ← ↑ → ↓ ↔ ↕, box drawing
// ─ │ ┌ ┐ └ ┘ ├ ┤ ┬ ┴ ┼ ═ ║ (5 columns wide with the 6th column blank, so a horizontal line is
// dashed) and the shapes ■ □ ▲ ▼ ○ ●. Every other code point, including every 3- and 4-byte scalar
// outside those blocks, is the replacement box (`has_glyph` says which). The tables for everything
// but ASCII are generated by `tools/font_gen.py` (accents are composed onto the
// ASCII letters, the rest drawn in that script) and are written below as hex-coded columns.
// Text is drawn at integer scales 1 to 4; a scale outside that panics rather than silently
// resampling, and the scaled coverage is built in a fixed stack mask (5 * 4 by 7 * 4 bytes, the 4x
// maximum), so nothing allocates and nothing is ever overdrawn.
//
// This module touches no pixel directly: it builds a coverage mask and hands it to
// `draw.blit_mask8`, which is the module that carries the `unsafe`. So `font.kp` needs no
// `[unsafe]` entry of its own, and the only thing it imports is the freestanding-clean
// `std/str` pair `len`/`byte` the compiler lowers itself.
impl "deps/polyp/backends/soft/soft/draw"
impl "deps/polyp/backends/soft/soft/fb"
impl "deps/polyp/backends/soft/soft/utf8"
impl "std/str"

pub const GLYPH_W: i32 = 5
pub const GLYPH_H: i32 = 7
pub const ADVANCE: i32 = 6
pub const MAX_SCALE: i32 = 4
